schema:
CREATE TABLE FOLLOW (
    FOLLOWEE VARCHAR(255),
    FOLLOWER VARCHAR(255),
    PRIMARY KEY (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER),
    CHECK (FOLLOWEE <> FOLLOWER)
);

counterexample:
-- Although one might imagine that duplicate relationships could yield a difference between counting all rows versus counting distinct followers,
-- the PRIMARY KEY (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) prevents such duplicates. As a consequence, no test data under the declared schema will make Q1 and Q2 return different results.

-- For illustration, here is some sample data that returns the same output in both queries:

INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('alice', 'bob');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('alice', 'charlie');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('bob', 'alice');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('bob', 'david');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('charlie', 'alice');

-- In this data:
-- • The set of users who appear as FOLLOWER is {bob, charlie, alice, david}.
-- • Q1 considers only rows where the FOLLOWEE is in that set. For example, 'alice' appears as FOLLOWEE in the first two rows and 'bob' appears as FOLLOWEE in the third and fourth rows.
-- • Q2’s join pairs F1 rows with F2 rows that have F2.FOLLOWER equal to F1.FOLLOWEE, and then counts distinct F1.FOLLOWER values per group.

-- Both Q1 and Q2 will return the same groups and counts on this data. Therefore, no counterexample exists under the imposed constraints.

