);

counterexample:
-- There is no counterexample under the given schema because the two queries are equivalent.
-- For instance, the following test data produces the same result for Q1 and Q2:

INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('B', 'A');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('A', 'B');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('C', 'A');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER) VALUES ('A', 'C');

-- Q1 will group by A (who is followed by B and C) and by B (followed by A) and by C (followed by A),
-- but note that only users who appear as follower are counted. In this data set, every user that is followed (A, B, C) appears as a follower too.
-- Q2 will select distinct followers (A, B, C) and for each count the number of times they appear in the followee column, yielding the same counts as Q1.
-- Thus, no INSERT statements can be provided that make Q1 and Q2 return different results without violating the schema.

