-- Both Q1 and Q2 will return the row for player 1 with EVENT_DATE '2020-01-01' and the row for player 2 with EVENT_DATE '2020-03-10'.

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2022-01-01', 100, 10);

-- The following two inserts simulate the case where the same player has two records on the day after installation.
-- (In a test/staging scenario the duplicate (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE) might occur if the primary key constraint is temporarily relaxed or if the data‐loading process has a bug.)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2022-01-02', 101, 5);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2022-01-02', 102, 3);

-- Optionally, add a second player who does not return the next day for control
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2022-01-01', 200, 8);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2022-01-01', 200, 2);

-- Sample test data (both queries return the same result with this data)
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2022-01-01', 1, 5);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2022-01-02', 2, 3);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2022-01-03', 3, 4);

-- The following INSERT statements produce a counterexample under the assumption that duplicate records on the same day are allowed (i.e. if the PK were not enforced or the data were not deduplicated).

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-01', 100, 5);
-- Two activity records for player 1 on the day immediately following his first activity
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 101, 3);
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2021-01-02', 102, 4);

INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-03', 200, 2);
-- Single consecutive-day record for player 2
INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2021-01-04', 201, 1);

/*
Under these data, the subquery in both queries will return:
  For player 1: MIN_DATE = '2021-01-01'
  For player 2: MIN_DATE = '2021-01-03'

Then:
  • Q1 joins back to ACTIVITY. For player 1 the joined rows include two rows with EVENT_DATE = '2021-01-02' (both satisfying MIN_DATE+1) so the SUM(CASE…) adds 2; while the denominator is COUNT(DISTINCT PLAYER_ID) = 2, giving a fraction of 1.00 (when rounded).
  • Q2 uses a LEFT JOIN on the condition FIRST_DATE = A2.EVENT_DATE-1. For player 1 the join finds (even if there are two matching rows) the aggregation of COUNT(A2.PLAYER_ID) is 2, but because the join duplicates the A1 row, the denominator also becomes 2, yielding 1.00; however, if one were to de-duplicate in the numerator (to count each player only once), Q2 would yield 0.50 if player 1 were counted only once. The key point is that the two queries differ in their treatment of duplicate consecutive-day records.

NOTE: The above counterexample relies on allowing duplicate activity records for the same player and day, which violates the declared PRIMARY KEY. In a strictly enforced schema the two queries will always produce the same result. This counterexample thus illustrates how a subtle difference in join conditions and aggregation can lead to divergence if the data are not “clean”.
*/

