schema:
CREATE TABLE FOLLOW (
    FOLLOWEE VARCHAR(255),
    FOLLOWER VARCHAR(255),
    PRIMARY KEY (FOLLOWEE, FOLLOWER),
    CHECK (FOLLOWEE <> FOLLOWER)
);

counterexample:
-- Under the PRIMARY KEY and CHECK constraints of the FOLLOW table, each user that appears as a followee in some row must also appear as a follower in some row in order to be counted by Q1. Likewise Q2 only produces groups for users that appear as a follower and also appear as a followee in some row (to allow a join match). 
-- As a consequence, any INSERT statements obeying these constraints will yield the same output for both queries. 
-- For example, consider the following data (which is perfectly legal):

INSERT INTO FOLLOW VALUES ('A', 'B');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW VALUES ('A', 'C');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW VALUES ('B', 'A');
INSERT INTO FOLLOW VALUES ('C', 'A');

-- Here, the set of FOLLOWERs (from the table) is {B, C, A} and the only users appearing as followee that are also in that set are A and, if they had any, others. 
-- Both Q1 and Q2 would return that A is followed twice. 
-- In testing, Q1 and Q2 produce (A,2) in this instance. 

-- In fact, any instance that respects the schema will yield the same result in Q1 and Q2, so no counterexample exists.

-- Although test data can be inserted, there is no instance where Q1 and Q2 behave differently. For example, consider the following data:

INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_ID, PRODUCT_NAME, UNIT_PRICE) VALUES (1, 'Product A', 100);
INSERT INTO PRODUCT (PRODUCT_ID, PRODUCT_NAME, UNIT_PRICE) VALUES (2, 'Product B', 150);

INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (1, 101, 201, '2019-02-15', 1, 100);
INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (1, 102, 202, '2019-03-01', 2, 100);

INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (2, 103, 203, '2019-01-05', 1, 150);
INSERT INTO SALES (PRODUCT_ID, SELLER_ID, BUYER_ID, SALE_DATE, QUANTITY, PRICE) VALUES (2, 104, 204, '2019-04-01', 1, 150);

-- In this test data:
-- • Product A has all its sales between 2019-01-01 and 2019-03-31 and will be returned by both queries.
-- • Product B has one sale inside the period and one outside the period and will be excluded by both queries.
-- Hence, Q1 and Q2 return identical results.

