counterexample:
-- As explained, no counterexample exists; regardless of the inserted rows, Q1 and Q2 produce the same final output.
-- For demonstration, here are a few INSERT statements that one might consider:

INSERT INTO MYNUMBERS (NUM) VALUES (NULL);
-- Q1: inner query returns no rows (because COUNT(NUM) is 0 for NULL), so MAX returns NULL.
-- Q2: inner query returns one row with NULL (because COUNT(*) is 1), and MAX(NULL) returns NULL.

INSERT INTO MYNUMBERS (NUM) VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO MYNUMBERS (NUM) VALUES (1);
-- Both queries will ignore the duplicated non-NULL value 1, so the final result remains NULL.

INSERT INTO MYNUMBERS (NUM) VALUES (2);
-- Now if we had a unique non-NULL value (say 2) along with a unique NULL row, Q1 returns 2 (ignoring the NULL group) and Q2 returns MAX(2, NULL) = 2.

-- In all cases, Q1 and Q2 output the same final value, so no counterexample can be provided.

