counterexample:
-- Although both queries produce the same result, here is an example of test data that meets the condition.

INSERT INTO ACTORDIRECTOR (TIMESTAMP, ACTOR_ID, DIRECTOR_ID) VALUES (1, 10, 20);
INSERT INTO ACTORDIRECTOR (TIMESTAMP, ACTOR_ID, DIRECTOR_ID) VALUES (2, 10, 20);
INSERT INTO ACTORDIRECTOR (TIMESTAMP, ACTOR_ID, DIRECTOR_ID) VALUES (3, 10, 20);

INSERT INTO ACTORDIRECTOR (TIMESTAMP, ACTOR_ID, DIRECTOR_ID) VALUES (4, 30, 40);
INSERT INTO ACTORDIRECTOR (TIMESTAMP, ACTOR_ID, DIRECTOR_ID) VALUES (5, 30, 40);

-- The pair (10,20) appears 3 times and will be returned by both queries, while (30,40) appears only 2 times and will be filtered out.

-- Since the semantics of GROUP BY and HAVING are the same as when used in a CTE, Q1 and Q2 will yield the same result on this data.

-- In summary, no counterexample exists where Q1 and Q2 differ.

