-- because the primary key (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE) prevents a player from having more than one record per day.
--
-- To illustrate what a counterexample might look like if duplicate day records were allowed (for example, if event_date were a datetime column instead of DATE), one could imagine data like:

-- Player 1: First login on 2020-01-01 and two activities on 2020-01-02
-- INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 10, 5);
-- INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02 09:00:00', 10, 3);
-- INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02 18:00:00', 10, 2);

-- Player 2: First login on 2020-01-01 and one activity on 2020-01-02
-- INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 20, 4);
-- INSERT INTO ACTIVITY (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE, DEVICE_ID, GAMES_PLAYED) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 20, 6);

-- In that hypothetical scenario (with event_date storing full timestamps and PK redefined accordingly),
-- Q1 would join player 1 to two day-1 events and contribute 2 toward the numerator while Q2’s LEAD would only pick the first subsequent event so count player 1 only once. 
-- This would cause Q1 to compute a higher day1_retention for 2020-01-01 than Q2.

-- However, given the current schema where event_date is DATE and the PK is (PLAYER_ID, EVENT_DATE), such a counterexample cannot be produced.

